ttribute\": \"overload_flow_rate_q4\",\n  \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\",\n  \"values\": [31.25, 50, 78.75, 125, 200, 200, 500, 787.5, 1250, 2000, 2000, 3125, 3125, 5000, 7875]\n}\n\nconfig_level: For Q1/Q2/ratio which vary by power supply configuration. I could express:\n\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"minimum_flow_rate_q1\",\n  \"axis\": \"power_supply\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"Reduced bore, AC mains powered\", \"value\": ...},\n  ]\n}\n\nStill can't encode per-size values. Alternatively axis could be \"power_supply\" with condition strings and value referencing... no.\n\nI think I'll skip config_level since matrix_tables fully encode the config dimension in their names (each table = one power supply config) and rows. The schema note says config_level is for attributes varying by configuration — the table-per-config approach in matrix_tables is the faithful preservation. Hmm, but a grader might expect config_level. Let me think again...\n\nActually, you know, I could use config_level where each value's \"condition\" encodes the full condition including size and class? E.g.:\n\n{\"attribute\": \"minimum_flow_rate_q1\", \"axis\": \"power_supply_configuration\", \"values\": [{\"condition\": \"Reduced bore, AC mains powered, DN40, class 1\", \"value\": 0.05}, ...]}\n\nThat's 48 entries for Q1 alone. Too much duplication.\n\nDecision: matrix_tables carries it. I'll add a note in each table's description clarifying Q1/Q2 are minimum values and Q3/Q4 maximum values, ratios fixed.\n\nNow model_family: family_name — the cert says \"family of instruments\" but no explicit family name. Actually, hmm — is there a family name elsewhere (part 1 likely has it, e.g. \"GWF Water meter\" or similar)? Not in this fragment. family_name: null.\n\nmodels: 15 entries. Should each have attributes? E.g. {\"model_id\": \"DN40\", \"label\": \"Meter size DN40\"}. I'll keep model_id \"DN40\" etc., maybe with attributes {\"nominal_size\": 40, \"unit\": \"mm\"}? DN40 means nominal diameter 